11:Recent neuroimaging studies and publicly disseminated analytic tools suggest that regional morphometric analyses covary for global thickness. We empirically demonstrated that this statistical approach severely underestimates regional thickness dysmorphology in psychiatric disorders. Study 1 included 90 healthy control participants, 51 participants at clinical high risk for psychosis, and 78 participants with early-illness schizophrenia. Study 2 included 56 healthy control participants, 83 participants with nonaffective psychosis, and 30 participants with affective psychosis. We examined global and regional thickness correlations, global thickness group differences, and regional thickness group differences with and without global thickness covariation. Global and regional thickness were strongly correlated across groups. Global thickness was lower in the schizophrenia spectrum groups than the other groups. Regional thickness de /uniFB01 cits in schizophrenia spectrum groups were attenuated or eliminated with global thickness covariation. Eliminating the variation that regional thickness shares with global thickness eliminated disease-related effects. This statistical approach results in erroneous conclusions that regional thickness is normal in disorders like schizophrenia or clinical high risk syndrome.
15:Whether to adjust for variance associated with global brain metrics is a perennial question in brain morphometry studies (1 -4). Recent studies in psychiatric disorders, including schizophrenia and bipolar disorder (4 -6), as well as the clinical high risk for psychosis (CHR-P) syndrome (7), have advocated that regional morphometric analyses covary for global mean thickness to support conclusions about disease-related regional speci /uniFB01 city. Moreover, recent publicly disseminated analytic tools for conducting ageand gender-normative modeling incorporate global covariation when deriving regional measurements without an option to omit global thickness covariation (8). These analytic approaches have been applied to large datasets, yielding seemingly de /uniFB01 nitive estimates of disease effects on region of interest (ROI) morphometry (5 -7). Multiple studies in which the global thickness variance was removed showed little to no disease effect on regional thickness pro /uniFB01 les (5 -7). However, this global thickness adjustment approach has some /uniFB02 aws (9), and in the current article, we statistically demonstrate how this approach leads to erroneous conclusions about thickness in psychiatric disorders.
17:Historically, neuroimaging studies have adjusted for head size or intracranial volume (ICV), thereby removing nuisance variance because somatic size variation, particularly ICV variation, is not typically affected by normal aging (10) or disease processes that systematically affect regional volumes or thickness. Because the variance removed is largely irrelevant to aging and/or disease effects on the brain, ICV-adjusted ROIs are more sensitive to these effects (1). Additionally, age-related ICV changes are present in children undergoing somatic growth, including head size and brain growth, which partially accounts for the observation that covarying for ICV reduces erroneous nonlinear effects of age from childhood to young adulthood (11). Interestingly, recent studies have documented ICV changes associated with older age (12,13), challenging the assumption that ICV is /uniFB01 xed in adulthood, although the effects have been small and the underlying mechanisms not fully understood. Notwithstanding these recent studies, it remains evident that global thickness, like the ROI thicknesses from which it is derived, is affected by normal aging (14,15) and disease processes (4,6,16 -19) to a much greater degree than ICV. Therefore, it is impossible to remove global thickness variance from ROI thickness measures without removing agingand disease-associated variance, rendering global thickness -adjusted ROI measures less
23:sensitive to these effects. Because ICV is less related to regional thickness, age, and disease-related processes, adjustment of regional thickness measures for ICV does not have the same deleterious impact as adjustment for global thickness.
25:For example, if a disease such as schizophrenia (4,6,16 -19) affects cortical thickness in a correlated, widespread manner, controlling for global thickness will attenuate or eliminate disease effects on regional thickness. In effect, the question being tested is not about disease-related regional speci /uniFB01 city, but rather, ' What would effects on thickness in a given region be if this region ' s thickness was uncorrelated with global thickness or if we only analyzed the aspects of regional thickness that are uncorrelated with global thickness? ' Given that global and regional thickness measures are strongly correlated, it is not clear that the scienti /uniFB01 c question addressed when using globally adjusted ROI thickness metrics is conceptually meaningful. Indeed, ROI thickness measures are so distorted when deprived of their shared variance with global thickness that disease effects are attenuated or eliminated, as shown in studies of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder (4 -6).
27:To provide empirical support for the above arguments, we examined regional thickness pro /uniFB01 les from 2 archival magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) studies. Study 1 included healthy control participants (HCs), participants at CHR-P, and participants with early-illness schizophrenia (ESZ) who underwent MRI scanning at the University of California San Francisco. Study 2 included HCs, participants with nonaffective psychosis (NAff-P), and participants with affective psychosis (Aff-P) who underwent MRI scanning as part of the HCP-EP (Human Connectome Project for Early Psychosis). We hypothesized that 1) covarying for global thickness would attenuate or eliminate patient group regional thickness abnormalities; 2) this would be especially true when there are patient group abnormalities in global thickness and when global and regional thickness are highly correlated; and 3) adjustment for ICV would not have the same detrimental effect.
49:Without Global Thickness. ROI thicknesses were regressed on age, gender, and site (only study 2 included site) in HCs to model the effects of normal aging, gender, and site. Resulting parameter estimates were used to derive predicted ROI values for each participant, irrespective of group, which re /uniFB02 ected the expected thickness for a healthy individual of a speci /uniFB01 c age, gender, and site. Regional thickness values were transformed to adjusted z scores (23 -25) (see Supplemental Methods) by subtracting predicted values from observed values and dividing by the HC model standard error of regression. Adjusted z scores represent each participant ' s deviation from the value expected for an HC of the same age, gender, and site.
