29:Clinical high-risk for psychosis (CHR-P) and early illness schizophrenia (ESZ) individuals were recruited from the early psychosis research clinic at University of California, San Francisco (UCSF). CHR-P participants met Criteria of Psychosis-Risk Syndromes (COPS) criteria as determined by the Structured Interview for Psychosis-Risk Syndromes (SIPS) (1-3). ESZ individuals ( n =78) met criteria for a current diagnosis of schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder as determined by the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-IV (SCID) (4). Healthy control (HC) individuals were recruited from the local UCSF community and did not meet Axis I diagnostic criteria as determined by the SCID 4 (for HC &gt;16 years old) or the Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia for School-Age Children, Present and Lifetime Version (for HC ≤ 16 years old) (5). All participants were required to be in good physical health, fluent in English, and between 12-35 years old. Participants were excluded if they had a past year DSMIV substance dependence diagnosis (excluding nicotine), or head injury with loss of consciousness, neurological illness.
33:HC, non-affective psychosis (NAff-P), and affective psychosis (Aff-P) individuals were scanned at one of three sites (Indiana University [ n =80], Brigham and Women's Hospital [ n =46], and McLean Hospital [ n =43]) as part of the Human Connectome Project-Early Psychosis (HCPEP) study. Diagnoses were determined by the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM5 -RV (SCID-5-RV) (6). NAff-P individuals ( n =83) met DSM-5   (6) criteria for schizophrenia ( n =58), schizophreniform ( n =9), schizoaffective ( n =11), psychosis not otherwise specified ( n =3), delusional disorder ( n =1), or brief psychotic disorder ( n =1). Aff-P participants ( n =30) met DSM-
37:5 criteria for major depression with psychosis (single and recurrent episodes; n =6) or bipolar disorder with psychosis (including most recent episode depressed and manic types; n =24). Inclusion criteria included: 1) 16-35 years old, 2) outpatients, 3) male or female, 4) fluent in English, 5) not pregnant, and 6) willing to share de-identified data. Exclusion criteria included: 1) substance-induced psychosis or psychotic disorder due to a medical condition, 2) IQ less than 70 based on medical history, 3) Human Immunodeficiency Virus positive (HIV+), 4) medical or neurological disorders affecting the central nervous system, 5) having a medical implant contraindicated for MRI, 6) current severe substance use disorder (excluding caffeine and nicotine), 7) past year electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) treatment, 8) at high suicidal risk, and 9) overly aggressive or pose a substantial risk of danger. HC individuals had no history of psychiatric hospitalization and were not taking psychiatric medications.
41:Study 1 participants were scanned at the University of California San Francisco's (UCSF) neuroimaging center using a Siemens 3T TIM TRIO scanner and high-resolution structural T1weighted MPRAGE image acquisition sequence: repetition time (TR)=2,300 ms, echo time (TE)=2.95 ms, flip angle= 9˚, field of view (FOV) =256mm, and slice thickness=1.20 mm.
